        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 1141,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 644,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Answers True only if EVERY value in a tensor is true (non-zero), and False as soon as a single one is zero.\n\nUse it to check that a condition holds everywhere: are all joints inside their range, did every sensor report, is every value finite, has every step finished. It turns a whole mask into one yes or no.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.all\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor - usually a mask from a comparison (t.gt, t.lt, ...). Every one that arrives is tested and the answer sent; it is the only inlet. Any shape is accepted and the WHOLE tensor is always tested: there is no dim, so you get one True or False, never one per row. A value counts as true when it is not zero - so negative numbers and NaN are true. A list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor first; an empty tensor is ignored (nothing is sent).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nall:\nTrue or False, as a 0-dimensional bool tensor (a toggle shows it).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1. t.gt compares each with the slider (which starts at 0.5) and draws the answer as a mask: white where the value is above the threshold, black where it is not. At 0.5 roughly half are black and t.all is False. Drag the slider down to 0: every cell turns white and the toggle lights - but only once the very last one, the smallest value, has turned. Click t.rand's button for new values.\n\nRELATED:\nt.any is the opposite question: true if ANY value is true. t.count_nonzero counts how many are true; t.all is that count equalling the number of cells, and asking whether t.min of the tensor is above the threshold is the same test.\nnp.all is the NumPy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 628,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Answers True only if EVERY value in a tensor is true (non-zero), and False as soon as a single one is zero.\n\nUse it to check that a condition holds everywhere: are all joints inside their range, did every sensor report, is every value finite, has every step finished. It turns a whole mask into one yes or no.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.all\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor - usually a mask from a comparison (t.gt, t.lt, ...). Every one that arrives is tested and the answer sent; it is the only inlet. Any shape is accepted and the WHOLE tensor is always tested: there is no dim, so you get one True or False, never one per row. A value counts as true when it is not zero - so negative numbers and NaN are true. A list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor first; an empty tensor is ignored (nothing is sent).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nall:\nTrue or False, as a 0-dimensional bool tensor (a toggle shows it).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1. t.gt compares each with the slider (which starts at 0.5) and draws the answer as a mask: white where the value is above the threshold, black where it is not. At 0.5 roughly half are black and t.all is False. Drag the slider down to 0: every cell turns white and the toggle lights - but only once the very last one, the smallest value, has turned. Click t.rand's button for new values.\n\nRELATED:\nt.any is the opposite question: true if ANY value is true. t.count_nonzero counts how many are true; t.all is that count equalling the number of cells, and asking whether t.min of the tensor is above the threshold is the same test.\nnp.all is the NumPy version."
        },
